        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.124,
            0.138,
            0.592,
            0.174
          ],
          "content": "**5.** In Fig. 6.20, DE || OQ and DF || OR. Show that EF || QR."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.124,
            0.179,
            0.592,
            0.232
          ],
          "content": "**6.** In Fig. 6.21, A, B and C are points on OP, OQ and OR respectively such that AB || PQ and AC || PR. Show that BC || QR."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.124,
            0.238,
            0.592,
            0.309
          ],
          "content": "**7.** Using Theorem 6.1, prove that a line drawn through the mid-point of one side of a triangle parallel to another side bisects the third side. (Recall that you have proved it in Class IX)."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.617,
            0.137,
            0.885,
            0.264
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.697,
                0.269,
                0.795,
                0.288
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 6.20"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_12_image_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.124,
            0.314,
            0.593,
            0.385
          ],
          "content": "**8.** Using Theorem 6.2, prove that the line joining the mid-points of any two sides of a triangle is parallel to the third side. (Recall that you have done it in Class IX)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.123,
            0.389,
            0.593,
            0.471
          ],
          "content": "**9.** ABCD is a trapezium in which AB || DC and its diagonals intersect each other at the point O. Show that ${ \\frac { \\mathrm { A O } } { \\mathrm { B O } } } = { \\frac { \\mathrm { C O } } { \\mathrm { D O } } }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.612,
            0.305,
            0.881,
            0.439
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.696,
                0.445,
                0.794,
                0.464
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 6.21"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_12_image_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.479,
            0.9,
            0.543
          ],
          "content": "**10.** The diagonals of a quadrilateral ABCD intersect each other at the point O such that ${ \\frac { \\mathrm { A O } } { \\mathrm { B O } } } = { \\frac { \\mathrm { C O } } { \\mathrm { D O } } }$ Show that ABCD is a trapezium."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.566,
            0.514,
            0.586
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "6.4 Criteria for Similarity of Triangles"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.593,
            0.9,
            0.632
          ],
          "content": "In the previous section, we stated that two triangles are similar, if (i) their corresponding angles are equal and (ii) their corresponding sides are in the same ratio (or proportion)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.637,
            0.463,
            0.655
          ],
          "content": "That is, in $\\Delta   \\mathrm { A B C }$ and   DEF, if"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.662,
            0.559,
            0.681
          ],
          "content": "(i) $\\angle \\mathrm{A} = \\angle \\mathrm{D}, \\angle \\mathrm{B} = \\angle \\mathrm{E}, \\angle \\mathrm{C} = \\angle \\mathrm{F}$ and"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.695,
            0.825,
            0.735
          ],
          "content": "(ii) $\\frac{\\mathrm{AB}}{\\mathrm{DE}}=\\frac{\\mathrm{BC}}{\\mathrm{EE}}=\\frac{\\mathrm{CA}}{\\mathrm{FD}}$ then the two triangles are similar (see Fig. 6.22)."
        },
        {
